-// Every x64 path to QPC ends in the x64 fast-forward sequence (FFS) of ntdll's hybrid export RtlQueryPerformanceCounter
-// (.hexpthk: `mov rax,rsp; mov [rax+20h],rbx; push rbp; pop rbp; jmp <EC body>`): kernel32!QueryPerformanceCounter is
-// `jmp [aux IAT]` to it, kernelbase / api-ms-win-core-profile forward to it, GetProcAddress(ntdll) returns it. FEX translates the
-// FFS like any x64 code. Under the key, the translation of the FFS's FIRST instruction begins with:
-//
-//     if (macrunner_ntdll_fast.qpc_enabled == 0) -> the unchanged translation of the FFS (jmp to the EC body = the old path)
-//     else: [rcx] = (counter + *base) * mul / div;  rax = 1;  ret
-//
-// with exactly the data PE ntdll's RtlQueryPerformanceCounter uses under MACRUNNER_QPC_FAST (QPC lane patch 0001: the EC half's
-// export `macrunner_ntdll_fast`, filled once by the unix side at load). The flag is read on every call, so the inline path is on
-// exactly when ntdll's own fast path is on (also under the hot switch) and the value is the one ntdll would compute: the same
-// counter register, the same commpage base (read every call: it moves on sleep / wake), the same 64-bit floor of x*mul/div
-// (as umulh by a magic constant, proven exact on ntdll's whole non-wrapping domain, see ComputeScale).
-//
-// Recognition is by address AND bytes: the block entry is the FFS address resolved from ntdll's export at process init, its 14 bytes
-// equal those seen at init (canonical FFS whose jmp lands on the EC body found through the CHPE redirection table), the EC body's
-// first 16 bytes are unchanged, and the page is not writable (FEX's write tracking then invalidates the block on any later patch).
-// A hooked IAT never reaches the FFS; a patched FFS fails the byte check; both take the old path.
-//
-// Keys (read once in ProcessInit): 0 (default) = nothing changes (no IR differs, the plan is never armed); 1 = on; 2 = on + count
